IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `roc-core`
IMPORT `roc-definitions`

-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), G.N. No. S 914/2021, current version as at 5 November 2026.
-- ORDER 3, RULES 3 AND 4: HOW A PERIOD IS COUNTED, AND WHEN THE COURT MAY EXTEND OR SHORTEN IT.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore Statutes Online.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here has been checked against the official text.
--
-- THIS MODULE ANSWERS THE SEAMS S-T1, S-T1b AND S-T2 of PLAN.md:
--   `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`            a DATE   (S-T1)
--   `o 3 r 3 the first day on which the act may be done` a DATE   (S-T1b)
--   `o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened`     MAYBE DATE (S-T2)
-- Other parts take those answers as inputs; a rule in THIS part calls the function.
--
-- WHY NO INTERPRETATION ACT MODULE.  Order 3, Rule 3(1) says "The Interpretation Act does not apply to the
-- calculation of time in these Rules".  So the staged Interpretation Act module (sgia-interpretation-time) is
-- not copied here, and the periods of this part are counted only by Order 3, Rule 3 (brief: "your text governs").
--
-- HOW A PERIOD IS READ HERE (the core's `Period` has a direction):
--   `After the anchor`  = "within N [days] after or from the date": the act is in time up to and including the
--                         last day this module returns (Rule 3(3): the period begins immediately after the date).
--   `Before the anchor` = "not less than N [days] before the date": the last day this module returns is the
--                         latest day on which the act may be done (Rule 3(4)).  A window "within N days before"
--                         a date begins on `o 3 r 3(4) the first day of a window within a period before a date`.
-- A DATE-valued function cannot say "the text does not cover this", so each has a yes/no twin that can:
--   `o 3 r 3 the period can be counted under this Rule`  (NOTHING for hours: the Rule counts days, weeks and months).

§§ `Order 3, Rule 3 -- calculation of time`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rule 3(1): the Interpretation Act does not apply
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:324 | The Interpretation Act does not apply to the calculation of time in these Rules.
@ref O 3 r 3(1) (src:324)
GIVEN `the time to be calculated is time in these Rules` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3(1) the Interpretation Act applies to the calculation of time in these Rules` MEANS
    IF `the time to be calculated is time in these Rules`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Interpretation Act does not apply to the calculation of time in these Rules"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the flat negative reaches "the calculation of time in these Rules" only

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rule 3(2): "month"
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:324 | (2) The word “month” means a calendar month unless the context otherwise requires.
@ref O 3 r 3(2) (src:324)
GIVEN `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3(2) the word month is read as a calendar month` MEANS
    IF `the context otherwise requires`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the context otherwise requires"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- A period of N calendar months after a date ends on the corresponding day N months later.
-- The Rules do not say where it ends when that month has no such day (31 January plus one month), so that case is NOTHING.
-- (daydate's `add months` clamps to the end of the shorter month; the clamped date is not the text's.)
-- src:324-325 | means a calendar month unless the context otherwise requires. (3) Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a
-- src:324-325 | specified date, the period begins immediately after that date.
@ref O 3 r 3(2) and (3) (src:324-325)
GIVEN anchor IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3(2) a period of calendar months from a date ends on the corresponding day` MEANS
    IF DATE_DAY (`add months` anchor months) EQUALS DATE_DAY anchor
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Rules do not say where a period of calendar months ends when the later month has no corresponding day

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rule 3(6): non-court days are left out of a short period.  The helpers first.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:328 | any day that is a non‑court day is to be excluded in the calculation of time.
@ref O 3 r 3(6) (src:328)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3(6) the day is a non-court day to be left out` MEANS
    CONSIDER `o 1 r 3(1) a day is a non-court day` day calendar
    WHEN JUST nonCourt THEN nonCourt
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE -- guard only: with a `Court calendar` the non-court-day answer is never NOTHING (KEEL-NOTES K15)

-- Step over `steps` court days from `start`, forwards or backwards, skipping non-court days.
-- A day counted is the one stepped onto; the start itself is never counted.
-- src:325-328 | the period begins immediately after that date. (4) Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified
-- src:325-328 | date, the period ends immediately before that date. (5) Where an act is required to be done a specified number of clear days before or after a
-- src:325-328 | specified date, at least that number of days must intervene between the day on which the act is done and that date. (6) If the period in question is 6
-- src:325-328 | days or less, any day that is a non‑court day is to be excluded in the calculation of time.
@ref O 3 r 3(3), (4) and (6) (src:325-328)
GIVEN start IS A DATE
      steps IS A NUMBER
      forwards IS A BOOLEAN
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` IS
    IF steps AT MOST 0
    THEN start
    ELSE
        IF `o 3 r 3(6) the day is a non-court day to be left out` (IF forwards THEN start PLUS 1 ELSE start MINUS 1) calendar
        THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` (IF forwards THEN start PLUS 1 ELSE start MINUS 1) steps forwards calendar
        ELSE `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` (IF forwards THEN start PLUS 1 ELSE start MINUS 1) (steps MINUS 1) forwards calendar

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rule 3(7): an expiry on a non-court day moves to the next court day
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:329 | expires on a non‑court day, the act is in time if done on the next day, not being a non‑court day.
@ref O 3 r 3(7) (src:329)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` IS
    IF `o 3 r 3(6) the day is a non-court day to be left out` day calendar
    THEN `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` (day PLUS 1) calendar
    ELSE day

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules 3(3) to (6): the count itself
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The day on which the period begins: "immediately after that date".
-- src:325 | (3) Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date.
@ref O 3 r 3(3) (src:325)
GIVEN anchor IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`o 3 r 3(3) the first day of a period after or from a date` MEANS anchor PLUS 1

-- The day before the date is where a period "before" a date ends: Rule 3(4).
-- src:326 | (4) Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified date, the period ends immediately before that
-- src:326 | date.
@ref O 3 r 3(4) (src:326)
GIVEN anchor IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`o 3 r 3(4) the last day of a period before a date` MEANS anchor MINUS 1

-- The first day of a window "within" N days before a date: N days back, counting only court days when N is 6 or less.
-- src:326-328 | (4) Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified date, the period ends immediately before that
-- src:326-328 | date. (5) Where an act is required to be done a specified number of clear days before or after a specified date, at least that number of days must
-- src:326-328 | intervene between the day on which the act is done and that date. (6) If the period in question is 6 days or less, any day that is a non‑court day is
-- src:326-328 | to be excluded in the calculation of time.
@ref O 3 r 3(4) and (6) (src:326-328)
GIVEN period IS A Period
      anchor IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A DATE
`o 3 r 3(4) the first day of a window within a period before a date` MEANS
    IF (period's `period unit` EQUALS Days OR period's `period unit` EQUALS `Working days`) AND (period's `period length` AT MOST 6 OR period's `period unit` EQUALS `Working days`)
    THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) FALSE calendar
    ELSE
        CONSIDER period's `period unit`
        WHEN Weeks THEN anchor MINUS (7 TIMES period's `period length`)
        WHEN Months THEN `add months` anchor (0 MINUS period's `period length`)
        WHEN Hours THEN anchor
        OTHERWISE anchor MINUS period's `period length`

-- Which periods this Rule can count: days, clear days, working days, weeks and months.  Hours it does not mention.
-- src:325-329 | (3) Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date. (4)
-- src:325-329 | Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified date, the period ends immediately before that date.
-- src:325-329 | (5) Where an act is required to be done a specified number of clear days before or after a specified date, at least that number of days must intervene
-- src:325-329 | between the day on which the act is done and that date. (6) If the period in question is 6 days or less, any day that is a non‑court day is to be
-- src:325-329 | excluded in the calculation of time. (7) Where the time prescribed by these Rules, or by any judgment, order or direction, for doing any act expires
-- src:325-329 | on a non‑court day, the act is in time if done on the next day, not being a non‑court day.
@ref O 3 r 3 (src:325-329)
GIVEN period IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3 the period can be counted under this Rule` MEANS
    CONSIDER period's `period unit`
    WHEN Hours THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): Rule 3 counts days, clear days, weeks and months; it says nothing about hours
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The last day for doing the act (seam S-T1).  `After the anchor`: within the period after or from the date.
-- `Before the anchor`: the latest day on which an act required "not less than" the period before the date may be done.
-- `Clear days` with `After the anchor` has no last day; the day returned is the EARLIEST day (Rule 3(5)).  See the fork register.
-- src:325-329 | (3) Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date. (4)
-- src:325-329 | Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified date, the period ends immediately before that date.
-- src:325-329 | (5) Where an act is required to be done a specified number of clear days before or after a specified date, at least that number of days must intervene
-- src:325-329 | between the day on which the act is done and that date. (6) If the period in question is 6 days or less, any day that is a non‑court day is to be
-- src:325-329 | excluded in the calculation of time. (7) Where the time prescribed by these Rules, or by any judgment, order or direction, for doing any act expires
-- src:325-329 | on a non‑court day, the act is in time if done on the next day, not being a non‑court day.
@ref O 3 r 3(3) to (7) (src:325-329)
GIVEN period IS A Period
      anchor IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A DATE
`o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` MEANS
    CONSIDER period's `period direction`
    WHEN `After the anchor` THEN
        CONSIDER period's `period unit`
        WHEN Days THEN
            `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days`
                (IF period's `period length` AT MOST 6
                 THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) TRUE calendar
                 ELSE anchor PLUS period's `period length`)
                calendar
        WHEN `Clear days` THEN
            IF period's `period length` AT MOST 6
            THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) TRUE calendar PLUS 1
            ELSE anchor PLUS period's `period length` PLUS 1
        WHEN `Working days` THEN
            `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days`
                (`o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) TRUE calendar)
                calendar
        WHEN Weeks THEN `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` (anchor PLUS (7 TIMES period's `period length`)) calendar
        WHEN Months THEN `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` (`add months` anchor (period's `period length`)) calendar
        WHEN Hours THEN anchor
    WHEN `Before the anchor` THEN
        CONSIDER period's `period unit`
        WHEN Days THEN
            `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days`
                (IF period's `period length` AT MOST 6
                 THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) FALSE calendar
                 ELSE anchor MINUS period's `period length`)
                calendar
        WHEN `Clear days` THEN
            IF period's `period length` AT MOST 6
            THEN `o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) FALSE calendar MINUS 1
            ELSE anchor MINUS period's `period length` MINUS 1
        WHEN `Working days` THEN
            `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days`
                (`o 3 r 3(6) the day reached by counting court days` anchor (period's `period length`) FALSE calendar)
                calendar
        WHEN Weeks THEN `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` (anchor MINUS (7 TIMES period's `period length`)) calendar
        WHEN Months THEN `o 3 r 3(7) the last day moved past non-court days` (`add months` anchor (0 MINUS period's `period length`)) calendar
        WHEN Hours THEN anchor

-- The first day on which the act may be done (seam S-T1b): the day after the date for a "within" period (Rule 3(3)),
-- and for a number of CLEAR days after the date the first day on which that many days have intervened (Rule 3(5)).
-- src:325-327 | (3) Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date. (4)
-- src:325-327 | Where an act is required to be done within or not less than a specified period before a specified date, the period ends immediately before that date.
-- src:325-327 | (5) Where an act is required to be done a specified number of clear days before or after a specified date, at least that number of days must intervene
-- src:325-327 | between the day on which the act is done and that date.
@ref O 3 r 3(3) and (5) (src:325-327)
GIVEN period IS A Period
      anchor IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
GIVETH A DATE
`o 3 r 3 the first day on which the act may be done` MEANS
    IF period's `period unit` EQUALS `Clear days` AND period's `period direction` EQUALS `After the anchor`
    THEN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` period anchor calendar
    ELSE anchor PLUS 1

-- Is the act in time?  The count above, then any extension or shortening (Order 3, Rule 4), then the day it was done.
-- A stated period: outside it the answer is JUST FALSE (ruling of 2026-11-05, (a)).
-- src:325 | Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date.
@ref O 3 r 3(3) (src:325)
GIVEN period IS A Period
      anchor IS A DATE
      `the day the act is done` IS A DATE
      calendar IS A `Court calendar`
      `o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3 the act is in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `o 3 r 3 the period can be counted under this Rule` period
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): Rule 3 does not count hours
    WHEN JUST counted THEN
        IF NOT counted
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): guard, the count never answers JUST FALSE
        ELSE
            IF period's `period unit` EQUALS `Clear days` AND period's `period direction` EQUALS `After the anchor`
            THEN
                IF `the day the act is done` AT LEAST `o 3 r 3 the first day on which the act may be done` period anchor calendar
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "at least that number of days must intervene between the day on which the act is done and that date" (a minimum gap, ruling of 2026-11-05 (a))
            ELSE
                IF `the day the act is done` AT MOST
                    (CONSIDER `o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened`
                     WHEN JUST extended THEN extended
                     WHEN NOTHING THEN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` period anchor calendar)
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "within a specified period after or from a specified date"

-- Two acts ("file and serve") are each in time, or one is not.  A false beats a nothing; a nothing beats a true.
-- src:325 | Where an act is required to be done within a specified period after or from a specified date, the period begins immediately after that date.
@ref O 3 r 3(3) (src:325)
GIVEN first IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      second IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 3 both of two acts are in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER first
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): one of the two acts is out of time
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN second
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER second
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): one of the two acts is out of time
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): one of the two acts cannot be decided

§§ `Order 3, Rule 4 -- extension or shortening of time`

-- Rule 4(1): the Court's power.  It reaches "the period within which a person is required by these Rules or by any
-- judgment, order or direction, to do any act in any proceedings" and no other period (a scope limit, no "only").
-- src:331 | The Court may extend or shorten the period within which a person is required by these Rules or by any judgment, order or direction, to do any act in
-- src:331 | any proceedings. (2) Unless these Rules otherwise provide, the Court may extend the period mentioned in paragraph (1) whether the application for
-- src:331 | extension is made before or after the expiration of that period.
@ref O 3 r 4(1) (src:331)
GIVEN `the period is one within which a person is required by these Rules, or by a judgment, order or direction, to do an act in proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 4(1) the Court may extend or shorten the period` MEANS
    IF `the period is one within which a person is required by these Rules, or by a judgment, order or direction, to do an act in proceedings`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power reaches "the period within which a person is required by these Rules or by any judgment, order or direction"

-- Rule 4(2): "Unless these Rules otherwise provide ...": a period for which they do is outside the power (the text's own "Unless").
-- Before or after the period expires makes no difference to the answer: the text says "whether ... before or after".
-- src:331 | (2) Unless these Rules otherwise provide, the Court may extend the period mentioned in paragraph (1) whether the application for extension is made
-- src:331 | before or after the expiration of that period.
@ref O 3 r 4(2) (src:331)
GIVEN `these Rules otherwise provide for this period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application for extension is made after the period has expired` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 4(2) the Court may extend the period whether the application is made before or after it expires` MEANS
    IF `these Rules otherwise provide for this period`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Unless these Rules otherwise provide"
    ELSE JUST TRUE -- the answer is the same whether the application is made before or after the period expires: "whether the application for extension is made before or after"

-- Rule 4(3): ONE extension by written consent, of at most 14 days, with no order of the Court.
-- The text limits the grant ("once", "a maximum period of 14 days"); outside it the answer is NOTHING, not JUST FALSE.
-- src:332 | (3) The period within which a person is required by these Rules, or by any order, to serve, file or amend any pleading or other document may be
-- src:332 | extended once by consent in writing for a maximum period of 14 days without an order of the Court being made for that purpose.
@ref O 3 r 4(3) (src:332)
GIVEN `the period is one within which a person is required by these Rules, or by an order, to serve, file or amend a pleading or other document` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the extension is by consent in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days by which the period is extended` IS A NUMBER
      `an extension by consent has already been made` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 3 r 4(3) the period may be extended by consent in writing without an order of the Court` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the period is one within which a person is required by these Rules, or by an order, to serve, file or amend a pleading or other document`
            THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph reaches a period "to serve, file or amend any pleading or other document"
        IF `the extension is by consent in writing` AND (NOT `an extension by consent has already been made`) AND `the days by which the period is extended` AT MOST 14
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may be extended once by consent in writing for a maximum period of 14 days without an order of the Court"

-- S-T2: the last day as extended or shortened.  NOTHING means "not extended or shortened by anything this module can see".
-- The Court's own order (Rule 4(1) and (2)) governs the day; a consent extension (Rule 4(3)) adds days to the last day under Rule 3.
-- src:331-332 | (1) The Court may extend or shorten the period within which a person is required by these Rules or by any judgment, order or direction, to do any act
-- src:331-332 | in any proceedings. (2) Unless these Rules otherwise provide, the Court may extend the period mentioned in paragraph (1) whether the application for
-- src:331-332 | extension is made before or after the expiration of that period. (3) The period within which a person is required by these Rules, or by any order, to
-- src:331-332 | serve, file or amend any pleading or other document may be extended once by consent in writing for a maximum period of 14 days without an order of the
-- src:331-332 | Court being made for that purpose.
@ref O 3 r 4 (src:331-332)
GIVEN `the last day under Order 3, Rule 3` IS A DATE
      `the day the Court has ordered the period to end` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the days by which the period was extended by consent in writing` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `an extension by consent had already been made` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day the Court has ordered the period to end`
    WHEN JUST ordered THEN JUST ordered
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER `the days by which the period was extended by consent in writing`
        WHEN JUST days THEN
            IF days AT MOST 14 AND (NOT `an extension by consent had already been made`)
            THEN JUST (`the last day under Order 3, Rule 3` PLUS days)
            ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "once ... for a maximum period of 14 days"
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): no order and no consent extension was made
